editorialMd:
Build the result list

Each booking is [start, end]. It uses time from start up to, but not including, end. Sort bookings by end time, from earliest to latest. For matching end times, put the earlier start first. Accept the first booking. Then accept a booking only if it starts at or after the end of the last one accepted. Record the length, end - start, of each accepted booking. Keep acceptance order. Negative times are allowed. With no bookings, the result list is empty.

Return the answer

Count the even numbers in the result list. An even number divides by 2 with no remainder. Count 0 and negative even numbers too. Return the count. An empty list gives 0.

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n log n); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

javascript
function bookingRackEvenKeyCensus(drawers, k) {
  const a=drawers; let out=[];
  const rows=a.map(r=>[...r]).sort((x,y)=>x[1]-y[1]||x[0]-y[0]);let end=-Infinity;for(const [s,e]of rows)if(s>=end){out.push(e-s);end=e;}
  return out.filter(x=>x%2===0).length;
}


